00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:07.430 align:start position:0%
the force of the impact  ripped apart these carriages  tearing and twisting the metal like a  tin can  the train was carrying nearly 500 people  at the start of a long holiday weekend  as it sped through the tunnel  dozens were pronounced dead at the scene  this is taiwan's deadliest rail accident  in decades  [Music]  this was believed to be the cause of the  derailment a construction vehicle parked  on a slope above the railway line  it slipped down from the work site and  hit the train  i was sitting on the aisle seat and i  was thrown to the floor  some survivors managed to walk out of  the tunnel along the top of the train  others had to be carried out  dozens received treatment at nearby  hospitals but the crumpled carriages  inside the tunnel made it difficult for  rescuers to reach those still trapped  more than 150 personnel were working at  the crash site  the trailer of the construction vehicle  slid off the road and crashed into the  train  now the whole head of the train is  damaged  the derailment happened on taiwan's  mountainous east coast a popular tourist  destination  especially as people left the cities  ahead of the tomb sweeping day holiday  taiwan's president cying wen said in  response to a train derailment in  hualien taiwan  our emergency services have been fully  mobilized to rescue and assist the  passengers  and railway staff affected we will  continue to do everything we can to  ensure their safety  in the wake of this heartbreaking  incident  everyone on the train has at least now  been accounted for  this is a day of immense grief for  taiwan  [Music]  the focus next will be on how this  terrible accident  was allowed to happen tom cheshire sky  news beijing
 

